    summary: "Preliminary results suggest that Kremlin-backed United Russia is on course to retain its constitutional majority.",
    preview: "Preliminary results from Russia's elections suggest that the party backed by President Vladimir Putin, United Russia, will maintain its grip on parliament. The outcome comes as no surprise, according to the BBC's Russia editor, Steve Rosenberg, who has been following the elections from Moscow. The vote comes after more than four and a half years of war against Ukraine, a conflict which has seen huge battlefield losses for the Russian army and sparked economic hardship.",
    body: "Preliminary results from Russia's elections suggest that the party backed by President Vladimir Putin, United Russia, will maintain its grip on parliament.\n\nThe outcome comes as no surprise, according to the BBC's Russia editor, Steve Rosenberg, who has been following the elections from Moscow.\n\nThe vote comes after more than four and a half years of war against Ukraine, a conflict which has seen huge battlefield losses for the Russian army and sparked economic hardship.\n\nThe only anti-war party, Yabloko, was removed the ballot in the run-up to polling day â although some candidates remained in some constituencies.\n\nRelated topics\n\n- RussiaUpdates from your News topics will appear in My News and in a collection on the News homepage.\n\n- EuropeUpdates from your News topics will appear in My News and in a collection on the News homepage.\n\n- War in UkraineUpdates from your News topics will appear in My News and in a collection on the News homepage.\n\n- Vladimir PutinUpdates from your News topics will appear in My News and in a collection on the News homepage.",
